README file for related scripts, add any explanations as necessary.

1. ImportingArcGIS:
This script imports the edited California_events.shp shapefile that was cut down to size in ArcGIS.

2. ProcessedDataScript:
This script goes through the California_events shapefile and edits the date format, 
cuts out extraneous columns, and saves the file into california_events_processed, so that it is easier to do work on it.

3. ExploratoryFigure:
This script makes an exploratory boxplot and scatter plot using ggplot2 that plot 
land cover and fire growth rate and year and fire growth rate respectively. In addition,
a data quality audit checking the dimensions of the california_events_processed file 
in relation to california_events.shp and checking how many na entries there are (3).

4. Week3Deliverable
In Week3 Deliverable, several statistical analyses are done on california_events_processed.
First, a two-way ANOVA (aov()) was done to explore if the predictors of land cover and month may cause
a variation in the response variable, mean growth of fire per day, with a boxplot using ggplot2 
illustrating the results. Second, an anova (aov()) exploring the relationship between 
time of year and fast fire length was run, with a boxplot to visually explore the model results.
Third, we produced a summary table (gt()) in order to illustrate the many different 
variables we had to work with and what variables guided our thought process. Then, we 
ran three anova models exploring the relationship between land cover and minimum, mean, and maximum 
fire growth rate. We also ran a q-q plot and a residuals vs. fitted plot to examine if 
an anova was even numerically appropriate here, which threw some red flags even when we
included a log transform. We ran tukey tests (TukeyHSD()) anyways, making a data table 
to illustrate the significant results. Lastly, we ran three gamma regressions (glm()) because 
we thought it might behave better with the data. We then assigned stars to significant P values
and made a point plot showing relative max, mean, and min fire growth rates for different
land cover types, with stars depending on statistical significance.

5. Week4Deliverable
In Week4Deliverable, we first 

->Time Series

Next, we run gamma models (glm()) on the land covers we found most interesting,
generating a more focused graphic than in Week3Deliverable. We then ran glm() gamma 
regressions where we regressed ecoregion onto fire growth rather than land cover type,
for min, mean, and max growth, to see if any geographical patterns emerged that simply
focusing on land cover might obscure. Lastly, we ran a gamma model that combines
land cover type and ecoregion (glm(mn_grw_km2 ~ lc_name + eco_name)) to see whether 
there were geographic differences in fire growth rate that land cover couldn't explain.
There were similar point plots for each of the gamma regressions here to keep things consistent.